WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.705
You tap Buy Now. A day or two
later, there's a box on your porch.

2
00:00:04.835 --> 00:00:06.727
Here's what happens in between.

3
00:00:08.077 --> 00:00:09.863
First, the inbound flow.

4
00:00:09.993 --> 00:00:12.568
Amazon forecasts what customers will want,

5
00:00:12.568 --> 00:00:16.613
and suppliers ship products into
a network of fulfillment centers,

6
00:00:16.613 --> 00:00:19.438
placed close to large groups of customers.

7
00:00:20.788 --> 00:00:23.540
Inside a fulfillment
center, products are stored,

8
00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:24.857
picked, and packed.

9
00:00:24.987 --> 00:00:29.397
Amazon bought the robot
maker Kiva Systems in 2012.

10
00:00:29.527 --> 00:00:32.879
Now mobile robots carry
shelves of products to workers,

11
00:00:32.879 --> 00:00:35.750
instead of workers
walking miles of aisles.

12
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.801
Then, the outbound flow.

13
00:00:38.931 --> 00:00:42.789
Packages move through sortation
centers and delivery stations,

14
00:00:42.789 --> 00:00:47.394
and much of the last mile is handled
by Amazon's own delivery network.

15
00:00:48.744 --> 00:00:50.402
Why does all of this matter?

16
00:00:50.532 --> 00:00:54.281
Speed and dependability create
time and place utility.

17
00:00:54.411 --> 00:00:56.709
A huge assortment creates variety.

18
00:00:56.839 --> 00:01:02.123
And keeping total logistics cost low
helps Amazon offer competitive prices.

19
00:01:02.253 --> 00:01:05.789
That's supply chain management
as a competitive advantage.

20
00:01:07.139 --> 00:01:11.869
Now answer the three case questions, and
compare your thinking with the key ideas.

